WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.600
&gt;&gt; Hi, I would like to introduce

00:00:24.601 --> 00:00:28.810
our open source platform
called OpenPAI.

00:00:28.811 --> 00:00:32.950
This is the landing page
of the platform.

00:00:32.951 --> 00:00:35.945
You can see that, right now,

00:00:35.946 --> 00:00:40.460
OpenPAI managed
more than 200 GPUs

00:00:40.461 --> 00:00:47.775
on Azure GPU VMs.

00:00:47.776 --> 00:00:51.450
From this page, you can see a set

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:55.320
of jobs running on top of PAI,

00:00:55.321 --> 00:01:00.630
and we can include

00:01:00.631 --> 00:01:05.520
a certain keyword to find
those jobs of interest.

00:01:05.521 --> 00:01:06.780
For example, in this case,

00:01:06.781 --> 00:01:09.760
I would like to find all the
jobs in the running stage.

00:01:09.761 --> 00:01:11.765
You can see that
right now there are

00:01:11.766 --> 00:01:14.050
more than 70 jobs
in running stage.

00:01:14.051 --> 00:01:20.225
I can also click

00:01:20.226 --> 00:01:25.715
one of the job to see the
overall information of the job.

00:01:25.716 --> 00:01:29.400
For example, this job
use four GPUs

00:01:29.401 --> 00:01:35.100
and they use one container,

00:01:35.101 --> 00:01:39.685
four GPUs, with
two parts available.

00:01:39.686 --> 00:01:42.800
Also, we can see

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:47.030
the job configuration
of these job and we

00:01:47.031 --> 00:01:50.420
can copy those job
configuration into

00:01:50.421 --> 00:01:51.800
a local file and use

00:01:51.801 --> 00:01:54.515
this configuration to
submit a job again.

00:01:54.516 --> 00:02:00.040
So, you can easily reproduce
this job on a cluster.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:08.150
We can also provide
some information for you to

00:02:08.151 --> 00:02:15.090
remotely log into this running
job to do remote debug.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:23.735
Also, we use this hardware page

00:02:23.736 --> 00:02:26.510
to monitor the overall GPU,

00:02:26.511 --> 00:02:29.300
CPU, essential hardware resource

00:02:29.301 --> 00:02:31.920
usage for this particular job.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.460
OpenPAI support the concept

00:02:40.461 --> 00:02:43.270
called a virtual cluster where

00:02:43.271 --> 00:02:45.455
multiple teams can share

00:02:45.456 --> 00:02:49.920
a physical cluster with
different virtual clusters.

00:02:49.921 --> 00:02:51.995
For example, in this cluster,

00:02:51.996 --> 00:02:56.125
we have three virtual clusters

00:02:56.126 --> 00:03:01.180
with different hardware
capacity configured.

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:06.830
We also provide
some monitoring tools

00:03:06.831 --> 00:03:12.935
to show the healthiness of
different PAI components.

00:03:12.936 --> 00:03:14.660
For example, in this case,

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:17.040
all the components shown in green

00:03:17.041 --> 00:03:22.740
means the PAI service
are in healthy state.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.655
We also provide
a user management.

00:03:26.656 --> 00:03:30.670
You can add new users and

00:03:30.671 --> 00:03:33.080
assign the user different
access permission

00:03:33.081 --> 00:03:35.700
to different virtual clusters.

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:41.125
If you are interested in PAI,

00:03:41.126 --> 00:03:43.565
please visit our GitHub website

00:03:43.566 --> 00:03:46.880
and we have a detailed document

00:03:46.881 --> 00:03:50.780
to introduce how to

00:03:50.781 --> 00:03:55.335
deploy and operate and
how to use OpenPAI.

00:03:55.336 --> 00:03:57.490
Thank you.

